-- StewardMD-authored monographs (exact doses; paraphrased prose; pending clinician review)
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO monographs (composition,generic_query,indication,dosage,pregnancy,specific_pop,adverse,interactions,warnings,forms,source,source_id,fetched_at) VALUES ('Etoricoxib','etoricoxib','Symptomatic relief of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis, and short-term relief of acute pain including acute gouty arthritis. A selective COX-2 inhibitor.','Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time. Osteoarthritis: 30 mg once daily, increased if needed to a maximum of 60 mg once daily. Rheumatoid arthritis: 60 mg once daily, up to a maximum of 90 mg once daily. Ankylosing spondylitis: 60 mg once daily (max 90 mg). Acute gout: 120 mg once daily, for no more than 8 days. Doses above those listed give no added benefit and are not recommended.','Avoid in pregnancy, especially the third trimester (risk of premature closure of the ductus arteriosus and impaired labour); not recommended while breastfeeding.','Mild hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh 5-6): do not exceed 60 mg once daily. Moderate impairment (Child-Pugh 7-9): do not exceed 60 mg every other day. Avoid in severe hepatic impairment and if creatinine clearance is below 30 mL/min.','Commonly gastrointestinal upset, ankle oedema, dizziness, headache, raised blood pressure and raised liver enzymes. As a COX-2 inhibitor it carries dose- and duration-related cardiovascular thrombotic risk and can still cause GI ulceration or bleeding.','Caution with anticoagulants and antiplatelet agents (bleeding), other NSAIDs and aspirin, ACE inhibitors/ARBs and diuretics (reduced blood-pressure control and renal risk), lithium, methotrexate, ciclosporin and tacrolimus. Alcohol adds to GI irritation.','Contraindicated in active peptic ulcer or GI bleeding, inflammatory bowel disease, severe hepatic impairment, CrCl <30 mL/min, established ischaemic heart disease, peripheral arterial or cerebrovascular disease, heart failure (NYHA II-IV) and uncontrolled hypertension (persistently >140/90). Check blood pressure within the first two weeks and monitor renal and hepatic function.','Film-coated tablets: 30 mg, 60 mg, 90 mg, 120 mg.','Compiled by StewardMD from EMA / ARCOXIA SPC & standard references — verify; pending clinician review.','','2026-06-28');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO monographs (composition,generic_query,indication,dosage,pregnancy,specific_pop,adverse,interactions,warnings,forms,source,source_id,fetched_at) VALUES ('Nimesulide','nimesulide','Second-line use only: acute pain, symptomatic treatment of painful osteoarthritis, and primary dysmenorrhoea.','Adults and adolescents over 12 years: 100 mg twice daily, taken after food. Maximum 200 mg per day. Use the lowest effective dose; continuous treatment must not exceed 15 days because of cumulative liver risk.','Contraindicated in the third trimester; avoid throughout pregnancy and during breastfeeding.','Contraindicated in hepatic impairment of any degree and in severe renal impairment (CrCl <30 mL/min). Not for children under 12 years.','Gastrointestinal upset and the usual NSAID effects; notably linked to a higher risk of serious, occasionally fatal, liver injury than several other NSAIDs — most hepatic events appear after about two weeks of use.','As with other NSAIDs: anticoagulants and antiplatelets, other NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors/ARBs and diuretics, lithium, methotrexate and ciclosporin. Avoid alcohol, which adds hepatic and GI risk.','Stop at once and check liver function if features of liver injury appear (loss of appetite, nausea, jaundice, dark urine, right-upper-abdominal pain). Do not co-prescribe other hepatotoxic drugs and do not exceed 15 days.','Tablets 100 mg (dispersible and suspension forms vary by region).','Compiled by StewardMD from EMA referral & NCBI LiverTox — verify; pending clinician review.','','2026-06-28');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO monographs (composition,generic_query,indication,dosage,pregnancy,specific_pop,adverse,interactions,warnings,forms,source,source_id,fetched_at) VALUES ('Aceclofenac','aceclofenac','Relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis, and other painful musculoskeletal, dental and gynaecological conditions. A phenylacetic-acid NSAID.','Adults: 100 mg twice daily, taken with or after food. Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration.','Avoid, particularly in the third trimester; not recommended during breastfeeding.','Use cautiously and at reduced exposure in renal or hepatic impairment and in the elderly; avoid in severe impairment.','Dyspepsia, abdominal pain, nausea, diarrhoea, dizziness and raised liver enzymes; the class NSAID risks of GI ulceration/bleeding and cardiovascular and renal effects apply.','Anticoagulants and antiplatelets, other NSAIDs and aspirin, ACE inhibitors/ARBs and diuretics, lithium, methotrexate and ciclosporin; alcohol increases GI risk.','Contraindicated in active peptic ulcer or GI bleeding, severe heart failure and severe hepatic or renal impairment. Cardiovascular and GI risk increase with dose and duration; monitor at-risk patients.','Tablets 100 mg; sustained-release 200 mg.','Compiled by StewardMD from standard pharmacology references — verify; pending clinician review.','','2026-06-28');
